<h2 id="why">Why dust needs a different tone</h2>
<p>Water and dust block a speaker in different ways. Water forms a film that a strong low note can burst. Dust and lint are made of many separate particles caught at different depths in the grille mesh, and each particle responds best to a slightly different kind of shaking. Very fine dust rattles most at higher pitches, while heavier specks and fibres respond to lower notes with bigger movement. A single 165 Hz tone helps, but it leaves some particles barely moving.</p>
<p>The dust cleaner on this page therefore plays a <strong>stepped tone band from 200 Hz up to 1500 Hz</strong>. It climbs through ten fixed steps, holding each for a little over two seconds, then descends back down through the same steps, and finishes with a short low flush at 165 Hz. Stepping, rather than gliding smoothly, creates a small jolt each time the pitch changes. Those jolts work like tapping a sieve: particles that are wedged against the mesh at one frequency are knocked loose when the next step arrives.</p>

<h2 id="band">What each part of the 200–1500 Hz band does</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Range</th><th scope="col">Effect on the grille</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>200–400 Hz</td><td>Large diaphragm movement, good for heavier specks, crumbs and loose fibres sitting on the surface.</td></tr>
<tr><td>500–800 Hz</td><td>The speaker plays these loudly and efficiently, giving strong vibration of the mesh itself.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1000–1500 Hz</td><td>Faster, smaller movements that shake very fine powder and pocket dust out of the tiny mesh holes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Final 165 Hz flush</td><td>A short burst of strong air movement that pushes out whatever was loosened.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The higher steps can sound piercing in a quiet room. That is normal. Use the tone level slider to keep it comfortable; even a moderate level is enough to vibrate the mesh, because higher frequencies are reproduced efficiently by small speakers.</p>

<h2 id="steps">How to clean dust from a phone speaker</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Look first.</strong> Use a torch to check the grille. If you can see a grey layer or fibres, deal with those mechanically before using sound.</li>
<li><strong>Brush gently.</strong> Use a soft, dry, clean brush such as a new soft toothbrush or a small paintbrush. Brush across the holes at a shallow angle, not straight into them.</li>
<li><strong>Lift, don't press.</strong> A small piece of low-tack tape or modelling putty dabbed lightly on the surface can lift lint. Do not press hard or leave residue behind.</li>
<li><strong>Set the volume.</strong> Media volume 60–90 percent is plenty for this mode. Remove the case.</li>
<li><strong>Run the dust cleaner.</strong> Tap Start with the grille facing down. The full routine takes just under a minute.</li>
<li><strong>Brush again.</strong> Loosened dust often collects at the edge of the grille. Give it a final light brush.</li>
<li><strong>Check the sound.</strong> Play speech or music at medium volume and listen for restored clarity in high notes.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="sources">Where speaker dust comes from</h2>
<h3>Pockets and bags</h3>
<p>Cotton pockets shed fibres every time the phone slides in and out. Over a few months these build a soft felt over the bottom grille and charging port. Phones carried in jeans pockets tend to collect the most.</p>
<h3>Workshops, building sites and cars</h3>
<p>Fine mineral dust from sanding, plaster or dirt roads is much smaller than lint and can work its way into the mesh holes. The higher steps of the dust band are particularly useful here.</p>
<h3>Makeup and skin oils</h3>
<p>Powders and oils from skin can combine into a sticky layer on the earpiece. Sound helps loosen it, but a gentle wipe with a dry microfibre cloth is usually needed as well.</p>

<h2 id="limits">When dust cleaning will not be enough</h2>
<p>If the grille is completely clogged with compacted lint, sound can only shake the surface. In that case careful brushing does most of the work and the tone finishes the job. If the speaker still sounds distorted after cleaning, or if you see damage to the mesh, the speaker itself may be worn or torn. Very old phones can also suffer from a degraded internal dust membrane. A repair shop can replace the speaker module on most phones for a modest price.</p>

<h2 id="routine">A simple maintenance routine</h2>
<p>Running the dust cleaner once a month, together with a quick brush, keeps lint from building up in the first place. Pair it with a check of the charging port, which collects the same fibres and can stop a cable from seating properly. A clean grille also helps the water eject mode work better the next time the phone gets wet, because water has fewer fibres to cling to.</p>
